UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 25, Lesson 1: Planning and making a class artwork', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"A class artwork is made by many people working together on one piece. First agree on a design brief: what the artwork shows, the size, the materials and the techniques, such as printing, collage or drawing. Divide jobs so everyone has a role, such as planning the layout, preparing the materials, making sections and putting the sections together. Draw a shared plan so every section fits. Listen to each other''s ideas, take turns and agree on one idea. Use one or two main colours so the artwork looks joined up.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nA group of three wants to make a class artwork of the ocean. Write the design brief in one sentence and give each person a job.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''We will make a collage of an ocean scene on one large sheet using blue, green and white paper. Person 1 plans the layout, person 2 makes the fish, person 3 makes the waves and plants.'' A clear brief and fair jobs help the artwork fit together.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking that group work means each person does their own picture on the same paper. A class artwork needs a shared plan, a common theme and a few shared colours so the parts join together.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nPlan a group artwork with a family member or friends. Write the design brief, list the materials and give each person a job. Draw the layout on a large sheet. Make your section and record how you listened to the other person''s idea.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Creative Arts","learning_intention":"A class artwork is made by many people working together on one piece. First agree on a design brief: what the artwork shows, the size, the materials and the techniques, such as printing, collage or drawing. Divide jobs so everyone has a role, such as planning the layout, preparing the materials, making sections and putting the sections together. Draw a shared plan so every section fits. Listen to each other''s ideas, take turns and agree on one idea. Use one or two main colours so the artwork looks joined up.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Explore and learn","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["CA2-VIS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Plan a group artwork with a family member or friends. Write the design brief, list the materials and give each person a job. Draw the layout on a large sheet. Make your section and record how you listened to the other pe Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Strong answer: ''We will make a collage of an ocean scene on one large sheet using blue, green and white paper. Person 1 plans the layout, person 2 makes the fish, person 3 makes the waves and… Reasoning: I used a class artwork by attending to a design brief rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Thinking that group work means each person does their own picture on the same paper. A class artwork needs a shared plan, a common theme and a few shared… Correction: Strong answer: ''We will make a collage of an ocean scene on one large sheet using blue, green and white paper. Person 1 plans the layout, person 2 makes the… Why: the correction restores accurate use of a class artwork.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking that group work means each person does their own picture on the same paper. A class artwork needs a shared plan, a common theme and a few shared colours so the parts join together. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating a class artwork on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with a class artwork, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Strong answer: ''We will make a collage of an ocean scene on one large sheet using blue, green and white paper. Person 1 plans the layout, person 2 makes the… This matches the lesson skill for a class artwork.","options":["Thinking that group work means each person does their own picture on the same paper. A class artwork needs a shared plan, a common theme and a few shared…","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work.","Strong answer: ''We will make a collage of an ocean scene on one large sheet using blue, green and white paper. Person 1 plans the layout, person 2 makes the…"],"question":"A group of three wants to make a class artwork of the ocean. Write the design brief in one sentence and give each person a job. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Thinking that group work means each person does their own picture on the same paper. A class artwork needs a shared plan, a common theme and a few shared… That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Strong answer: ''We will make a collage of an ocean scene on one large sheet using blue, green and white paper. Person 1 plans the layout, person 2 makes the…","Careful use of a design brief with clear evidence.","Thinking that group work means each person does their own picture on the same paper. A class artwork needs a shared plan, a common theme and a few shared…","A class artwork is made by many people working together on one piece. First agree on a design brief: what the artwork shows, the size, the materials and the…"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising a class artwork?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named a design brief, not by copying the model.","options":["Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Invent a sacred First Nations design without permission or guidance.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the a design brief used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence."],"question":"A learner is practising a class artwork. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Strong answer: ''We will make a collage of an ocean scene on one large sheet using blue, green and white paper. Person 1 plans the layout, person 2 makes the… uses elements, techniques and audience effect appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Strong answer: ''We will make a collage of an ocean scene on one large sheet using blue, green and white paper. Person 1 plans the layout, person 2 makes the…","Thinking that group work means each person does their own picture on the same paper. A class artwork needs a shared plan, a common theme and a few shared…","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work."],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about a class artwork?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Thinking that group work means each person does their own picture on the same paper. A class artwork needs a shared plan, a common theme and a few shared…","Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about a class artwork.","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue.","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about a design brief?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Example: a class of four plans a mural of a rainforest. Brief: one large paper, collage and printed leaves. Jobs: Ava draws the layout,…","Thinking that group work means each person does their own picture on the same paper. A class artwork needs a shared plan, a common theme and a few shared…","Invent a sacred First Nations design without permission or guidance."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — A class artwork is made by many people working together on one piece. First agree on a design brief: what… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete.","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every online claim as equally reliable."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work.","Thinking that group work means each person does their own picture on the same paper. A class artwork needs a shared plan, a common theme and a few shared…","Plan a group artwork with a family member or friends. Write the design brief, list the materials and give each person a job. Draw the layout on a…","Retype the worked example with no new example."],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of a class artwork?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating a class artwork on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Strong answer: ''We will make a collage of an ocean scene on one large sheet using blue, green and white paper. Person 1 plans the layout, person 2 makes the fish, person 3 makes the waves and… Reasoning: I used a class artwork by attending to a design brief rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with a class artwork, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Thinking that group work means each person does their own picture on the same paper. A class artwork needs a shared plan, a common theme and a few shared… Correction: Strong answer: ''We will make a collage of an ocean scene on one large sheet using blue, green and white paper. Person 1 plans the layout, person 2 makes the… Why: the correction restores accurate use of a class artwork.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-arts-y3-w25-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Creative Arts alignment using catalogue outcome codes (CA2-*); content-point mapping remains pending. First Nations arts content requires local Aboriginal educator or Custodian guidance before acceptance; do not invent sacred designs.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise a class artwork.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Visual Arts","success_criteria":["I can use a class artwork with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify a design brief and explain why it matters.","I can practise roles independently on a new prompt.","I can notice a shared plan needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"A class artwork is made by many people working together on one piece. First agree on a design brief: what the artwork shows, the size, the materials and the techniques, such as printing, collage or drawing. Divide jobs so everyone has a role, such as planning the layout,…","options":["A class artwork is made by many people working together on one piece. First agree on a design brief: what the artwork shows, the size, the materials and the techniques, such as printing, collage or drawing. Divide jobs so everyone has a role, such as planning the layout, preparing the materials, making sections and putting the sections together. Draw a shared plan so every section fits. Listen to each other''s ideas, take turns and agree on one idea. Use one or two main colours so the artwork looks joined up.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a class artwork?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: a class of four plans a mural of a rainforest. Brief: one large paper, collage and printed leaves. Jobs: Ava draws the layout, Ben prints leaf patterns, Cleo collages the animals and Dev adds the border. They agree on greens, yellows and one red for the parrot, so the mural looks connected. They check the plan each lesson to see that each section fits.\n\nTRY FIRST\nA group of three wants to make a class artwork of the ocean. Write the design brief in one sentence and give each person a job.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''We will make a collage of an ocean scene on one large sheet using blue, green and white paper. Person 1 plans the layout, person 2 makes the fish, person 3 makes the waves and plants.'' A clear brief and fair jobs help the artwork fit together.","explain":"A class artwork is made by many people working together on one piece. First agree on a design brief: what the artwork shows, the size, the materials and the techniques, such as printing, collage or drawing. Divide jobs so everyone has a role, such as planning the layout, preparing the materials, making sections and putting the sections together. Draw a shared plan so every section fits. Listen to each other''s ideas, take turns and agree on one idea. Use one or two main colours so the artwork looks joined up.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking that group work means each person does their own picture on the same paper. A class artwork needs a shared plan, a common theme and a few shared colours so the parts join together. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"A design brief and jobs.","caption":"Agree before you start.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Brief</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">what, size, materials</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Jobs</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">plan, print, collage, border</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: a class of four plans a mural of a rainforest. Brief: one large paper, collage and printed leaves. Jobs: Ava dr Then write your own artistic reason.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.\nJuno: Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: a class of four plans a mural of a rainforest. Brief: one large paper, collage and printed leaves. Jobs: Ava dr Then write your own artistic reason."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a class artwork?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: a class of four plans a mural of a rainforest. Brief: one large paper, collage and printed leaves. Jobs: Ava draws the layout, Ben prints leaf patterns, Cleo collages the animals and Dev adds the border. They agree on greens, yellows and one red for the parrot, so the mural looks connected. They check the plan each lesson to see that each section fits. Look at each line, sound, movement or role choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key artistic choices from the example and say what each one does for the audience or artwork.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful response names the element or technique and its effect, not only “it looks nice”.","explain":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final praise word without naming a choice does not show arts thinking. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Show the artistic choice","visual":[{"alt":"Shared colours and a plan.","caption":"Join the parts.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Shared colours</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">greens, yellows, one red</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Plan</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">every section fits</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a class artwork?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThinking that group work means each person does their own picture on the same paper. A class artwork needs a shared plan, a common theme and a few shared colours so the parts join together. Here is a more helpful example: Example: a class of four plans a mural of a rainforest. Brief: one large paper, collage and printed leaves. Jobs: Ava draws the layout, Ben prints leaf patterns, Cleo collages the animals and Dev adds the border. They agree on greens, yellows and one red for the parrot, so the mural looks connected. They check the plan each lesson to see that each section fits.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice weakens the work, then write a stronger choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''We will make a collage of an ocean scene on one large sheet using blue, green and white paper. Person 1 plans the layout, person 2 makes the fish, person 3 makes the waves and plants.'' A clear brief and fair jobs help the artwork fit together.","explain":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the decoration without changing the artistic idea does not fix the error. Thinking that group work means each person does their own picture on the same paper. A class artwork needs a shared plan, a common theme and a few shared colours so the parts join together. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["A guess with no artistic reason.","Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a class artwork?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA class artwork is made by many people working together on one piece. First agree on a design brief: what the artwork shows, the size, the materials and the techniques, such as printing, collage or drawing. Divide jobs so everyone has a role, such as planning the layout, preparing the materials, making sections and putting the sections together. Draw a shared plan so every section fits. Listen to each other''s ideas, take turns and agree on one idea. Use one or two main colours so the artwork looks joined up. Now choose a different subject or piece to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same arts idea as the model, but with different content or material.","explain":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new artwork or performance. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Transfer to a new artwork or performance"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 25, Lesson 1: Planning and making a class artwork","unit_number":5,"unit_title":"Printmaking, collage and texture","week":25,"week_in_unit":5,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: a class of four plans a mural of a rainforest. Brief: one large paper, collage and printed leaves. Jobs: Ava draws the layout, Ben prints leaf patterns, Cleo collages the animals and Dev adds the border. They agree on greens, yellows and one red for the parrot, so the mural looks connected. They check the plan each lesson to see that each section fits.\n\nWrite what makes this example effective, naming the element, technique or performance choice and its effect.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"A design brief and jobs.","caption":"Agree before you start.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Brief</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">what, size, materials</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Jobs</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">plan, print, collage, border</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Shared colours and a plan.","caption":"Join the parts.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Shared colours</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">greens, yellows, one red</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Plan</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">every section fits</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-arts-y3-w25-l1' AND family_id IS NULL;
